<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1447">1447</a>–<a href="/years/1508">1508</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Albert the Wise · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert IV, called the Wise, was Duke of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> from 1465. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-landshut-succession">War of the Landshut Succession</a> of 1503–1505 he reunited the divided Wittelsbach duchies into a single Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and in 1506 he decreed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a> so that the land would not be partitioned again. Munich became the permanent capital, and the Frauenkirche was completed early in his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-iv-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barthel Beham · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albert_IV,_Duke_of_Bavaria,_portrait_by_Barthel_Beham.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert IV was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 15 December 1447, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iii-duke-of-bavaria">Albert III, Duke of Bavaria</a>, and Anna of Brunswick-Grubenhagen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einbeck">Einbeck</a>. He was intended for a spiritual career, but after the death of his older brother John IV, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, he gave it up and returned from Pavia to Munich. When his brothers Christoph and Wolfgang resigned, Albert became sole duke of Bavaria-Munich, although a new duchy of Bavaria-Dachau was created out of it for his brother Sigismund. It reverted to Bavaria-Munich after Sigismund's death in 1501.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Albert's marriage to Kunigunde of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, a daughter of Emperor Frederick III, came out of intrigue. He illegally took control of some imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> and then asked to marry Kunigunde, who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a> far from her father, offering the fiefs as her dowry. Frederick agreed at first but withdrew his consent after Albert seized another fief, Regensburg. The wedding took place in January 1487, before the change of heart could be communicated to her. Frederick's son Maximilian I mediated and a war was prevented. In 1491–1492 Albert fought the rebellious Löwlerbund, and in 1492 he returned his acquisitions in Swabia to avoid war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a>.</p><p>After George, the last duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a>, died in 1503, Albert reunited the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in a war against George's heirs, the Palatinate line of the Wittelsbach family. As compensation for Maximilian's support he had to hand his brother-in-law the southernmost districts of Bavaria-Landshut: Kufstein, Kitzbühel and Rattenberg passed to Maximilian in 1506 and were united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>. For the Palatinate branch a new duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> was created.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>To prevent any future division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, Albert decreed in 1506 that the firstborn prince should always succeed. His oldest son and successor, William IV, nevertheless had to share power from 1516 with his younger brother Louis X, Duke of Bavaria, and the decree took effect only after Louis died in 1545. It remained in force until the end of the Bavarian monarchy in 1918. Albert and Kunigunde had eight children. He died on 18 March 1508 and is buried in the Frauenkirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert IV of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The War of the Landshut Succession of 1503 to 1505 gave most lands to <b>Albert IV of Bavaria-Munich</b>, while Palatinate-Neuburg was created for Rupert's sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Albert IV</b>, duke from 1465, built a stronger, more centralised state, and Munich's Frauenkirche was completed under his rule in 1488.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-munich">Duchy of Bavaria-Munich</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1486" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1486">What was here in 1486? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
